          "description": "Young local talents join Newcrest Lihir operation.\n\nNewcrest Lihir in New Ireland province has welcomed six young local talents (Lihirians) into its mining operation, after completing their training and receiving certificates and licenses to operate haul trucks.\n\nLihir's Geotechnical Superintendent James Bu welcomed the talents and praised them and their trainers for being resilient during their 12-month training program despite COVID-19 restrictions and challenges.\n\nThese Lihirian talents are Elizah Penias, Aaron Toarino, Cecilia Aeh, Dorothy Ziksaranie, John Liu, and Gerard Anap.\n\nThey completed an initial three-month training program that included simulator training, familiarisation on haul trucks, safe operating procedures, and theory assessments before proceeding into mine operations for real training experiences.\n\n\"As a business, we are proud of their achievement; it reflects Newcrest's commitment to upskilling local talents (Lihirians) and Papua New Guineans to take up roles within the operation,\" said Mr Bu.\n\nHe highlighted that this achievement also reflects the success of Newcrest Lihir's Work Ready Program; a training program that prepares Lihirians for the workplace.\n\"Our Work Ready Program empowered the talents with skills such as time management, public speaking, work standards and ethics, innovation and problem solving and safety. These skills helped them to focus and succeed in their training program with our mine department,\" Mr Bu said.\n\nSince 2019, Lihir's mine department has trained and recruited 12 Lihirians who had initially trained under the Work Ready Program.\nFurthermore, Lihir's Mine Production Superintendent Noven Giuna said the Work Ready program is crucial to the operation.\n\n\"It is building a diverse workforce and is setting us in the right direction, not just for Lihir, but for PNG,\" Mr Giuna said.\n\nCecilia Aeh, one of two women among the new technicians, described her achievement has 